.\" Man page for ax25netctl
.\"
.TH AX25NETCTL 8 "06 Oct 2026" "ax25-apps" "Linux System Administration"
.SH NAME
@@@ax25netctl@@@ \- say what a port name is
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B @@@ax25netctl@@@
[\-L] [\-H \fIendpoint\fR] [\-C \fIfile\fR] [\-v] [\fIname\fR ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B @@@ax25netctl@@@
answers one question about the ports of this machine: what
.I name
is, and can it be used.
.PP
A name in
.BR axports (5)
says that a port exists.  It does not say which stack serves it, and it does
not say whether the thing behind it is there.  A name can be a kernel AX.25
interface, a port of a WAMPES node, or a channel of an
.BR ax25netd (8)
upstream, and a program that gets it wrong sends on a radio the operator never
named.  The numbering of the third case is the server's: an upstream at
position
.I i
of
.BR ax25netd_agwpe.conf (5)
owns ports
.I i
*16 to
.I i
*16+15, and which
.I i
a given upstream has is written down nowhere but in the server's own port
table.
.PP
So
.B @@@ax25netctl@@@
asks the library what a name is, and then asks
.BR ax25netd (8)
whether it agrees.
.PP
With no
.IR name ,
one line is printed for every entry of the
.BR axports (5)
file.  With
.IR name s,
each is printed in detail, and a name that cannot be used is followed by the
reason.  The exit status is 0 when every name given resolved to a port that
the server has, and 1 otherwise; with no names it is 1 only when the tables
could not be read or the server could not be reached.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-L
Read the local configuration only and do not contact
.BR ax25netd (8).
Faster, and the answer is then what the files say and not whether an upstream
is connected.  The
.B IN NETD
column becomes
.BR ? ,
which means the question was not asked and is not an answer of no.
.TP
.BI \-H " endpoint"
The
.BR ax25netd (8)
to ask: a path for its unix domain socket, or
.IB host : port
for its TCP loop port.  This overrides
.BR @@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/ax25common.conf (5)
and the
.B AXSOCK_HOST
environment variable, and is also what the ports are resolved against, so that
the library and the server are always the same two.  See
.BR ENVIRONMENT .
.TP
.BI \-C " file"
Shared loop port configuration, read by
.BR ax25netd (8),
.BR @@@ax25netctl@@@
and the AGWPE shim alike.  Default
.IR @@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/ax25common.conf .
.TP
.B \-v
Also print what
.BR axports (5)
says about each name.  For a name that has no entry of its own this is often
the whole answer.
.TP
.B \-h
Print a usage message and exit.
.SH NAMED PORTS
.I name
is what
.BR axports (5)
calls a port.  A channel may be given as
.IB port / channel \fR,
which is the notation
.BR ax25tcpd (8),
.BR axkill (8)
and
.BR axctl (8)
take on a command line:
.B hf/2
is channel 2 of the upstream
.BR hf .
.PP
.BR axports (5)
itself writes the channel behind a colon, as
.BR hf:2 ,
and that is what the lookup uses.  A port name contains no slash, so there is
nothing the split could be ambiguous with.
.PP
A suffix behind the slash must be a number from 0 to 15, and anything else is
refused rather than taken as channel 0: an upstream has sixteen channels, and
a name that cannot mean one of them is a mistake worth reporting.
.SH OUTPUT
The table without names has one row per
.BR axports (5)
entry:
.TP
.B NAME
The port name as written in the file.
.TP
.B CALL
The call sign of the entry, or
.B \-
for a name that has no entry of its own.
.TP
.B BACKEND
.TP
.B PORT
What serves the name:
.BR kernel ", " wampes " or " agwpe .
For
.B agwpe
the flat port byte, which is the server's own numbering and the byte that goes
into a frame header.  The channels of one upstream are contiguous, so the port
number alone does not say which radio it is \-
.TP
.B CHAN
for
.B agwpe
the channel of that port, 0 to 15.
.TP
.B UPSTREAM
for
.B agwpe
the name the server knows the upstream by, as in
.BR ax25netd_agwpe.conf (5);
for
.B wampes
the name of the node.
.TP
.B IN NETD
.TP
.B DESCRIPTION
.B yes
when
.BR ax25netd (8)
lists that port,
.B NO
when it does not,
.BR ? ,
when it was not asked, and
.B \-
for a name that is not a port of the server.  A
.B NO
means that nothing sent on the port can leave: the upstream is down, has not
answered the port list request yet, or is not in
.BR ax25netd_agwpe.conf (5).
The description from the
.BR axports (5)
line comes last, and is not cut to a fixed width.
.PP
After the table comes what the server said about itself: its endpoint, its
version, and its own port table as the server wrote it.
.PP
With
.I name s
each name gets a block instead, which says the same thing in full and adds the
reason when there is nothing to be had.  A name that resolves to an AGWPE port
the server does not list is answered with what to look at; a name that is not
an AGWPE port is answered with the stack it belongs to, or, if none, with the
line the colon would have to be behind.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B AXSOCK_HOST
The endpoint the AGWPE shim uses, which
.B @@@ax25netctl@@@
reads as well and which
.B \-H
overrides.  A leading
.B /
means a unix domain socket; anything else is
.IB host : port \fR.
Set before anything asks the library a question, so that the ports are resolved
against the same server this tool reports on.  See
.BR axsock (7).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I @@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/axports
The port list, one line per port.
.TP
.I @@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/ax25common.conf
Where the loop port is, and which accounts may connect to it.
.TP
.I @@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/ax25netd_agwpe.conf
The upstream list of an
.BR ax25netd (8)
on this machine, which decides a port number when the server cannot be asked.
.TP
.I @@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/wampes.conf
The WAMPES nodes this machine can reach.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when every name given resolved to a port the server has, or \-
with no names \-
when the tables could be read.  1 when a name did not resolve, when it
resolved to a port the server does not have, or when the server could not be
reached.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B ax25netctl
Every configured port, and what
.BR ax25netd (8)
says about each.  This is the first thing to read when a connect fails.
.TP
.B ax25netctl hf/2 -v
Everything about channel 2 of the upstream
.BR hf ,
including the
.BR axports (5)
line, if there is one.
.TP
.B ax25netctl -L
Only what the configuration files say, with no server contacted.  Useful where
the socket is not readable by this account, and as the other half of a
comparison with a run without
.BR \-L :
what a name resolves to locally and what the server says about it are the two
things that can differ.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ax25netd (8),
.BR ax25netd_agwpe.conf (5),
.BR ax25common.conf (5),
.BR axports (5),
.BR ax25tcpd (8),
.BR axkill (8),
.BR axctl (8),
.BR axsock (7),
.BR call (1),
.BR wampes.conf (5)